  body: `
# Unit 6, CREATE, Writing Lab: "Inspire" (Build Foundation Session 18, Story Guide pp. 112–116)
Aaron's teaching: Story of Self is a rite of passage. We don't end our story with "and they lived happily ever after." We end it with a Call to Action. All we truly leave behind is our story; this is our legacy. Despair (without spirit) becomes INSPIRED (with spirit). The last paragraph, Inspire, is written TO THEMSELVES: a gift and a letter to self. It's the final call to who they are and want to become, the choices they'll make, the growth they see, and an acknowledgment of the pain they've been through and the purpose they've gained. Keanu Reeves, asked what happens when we die: "I know that the ones who love us will miss us."

STEP connector: Ask Aaron's question: "What's one thing you want the people who love you most to say about you?" Frame it, as he does, not as a question about death but about how they choose to live.
STEP legacy: Ask Aaron's four, one at a time: "Who am I choosing to be in this life?" "What will I choose to value, what will I aim at now?" "What commitments am I making to myself and others?" "How will I use my story to contribute to the world?" Then: "What on that list both challenges you and inspires you?"
STEP draft_inspire: Writing lab: the final paragraph, written to themselves, in a few sentences, in their own words. Aaron's own example (to share only if they're stuck): "I am no longer invisible, today I can see my own potential. I choose to live each day, not in the fear of others' judgement, but in the courage to seek and find my own exceptional story…" Capture draft_inspire.
STEP call: Show the whole Call to Action together: Innovate → Involve → Inspire. Ask if they want to adjust anything so it flows as one closing. Capture any revised drafts.
STEP close: Celebrate finishing Unit 6. Every piece of their story now exists. Encourage a few days of letting it eat. Preview Unit 7, Close the Circle: polishing the whole story and telling it.`,
